\f1 \cf9 \cb10 \uc0\u10095 
\f0  \cf11 You both look up, spotting a PYTHON slithering through the branches. It knocks loose an APPLE that falls, as if in suspended gravity or in slow motion, bouncing off the JUDGE's\cf2  \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11  head... "By Newton," the Judge says, rubbing the bump, "what an OMEN..."\cf2                                                                                                         \cb3 \
                                                                                                                                                                                   \

\f2 \cf11 \uc0\u9210 
\f0 \cf2  She finishes helping him up.                                                                                                                                                     \
                                         \
  She looks at the apple on the ground. She looks at the python, which continues through the branches with the sovereign indifference of a creature that has knocked loose         \
  something important and does not intend to explain itself. It was not the mogget. It was never going to be the mogget. It was something older.\
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  Python. Delphi's guardian. The great serpent at the oracle's threshold, slain by Apollo, name given to the Pythia \'97 the prophetess who breathed vapor and spoke in riddles and   \
  was almost always right if you knew how to read her. The oracle's serpent, still in trees, still dispensing prophecy by accident.\
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  The apple sits in the dust. Round. Unremarkable. Fallen.                                                                                                                         \
   \
  By Newton.                                                                                                                                                                       \
                                                            \
  She almost smiles.                                                                                                                                                               \
   \
  Newton and his apple: gravity asserting itself, making itself known to a man who was watching carefully enough to understand what he witnessed. Not the apple falling \'97 apples   \
  had always fallen. The recognition of the law. The law was already there. The apple merely demonstrated it.\
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  And ahead of them, waiting at the Hotel, in the dock: GEMINIAEUS. The Antigravity Lich. The entity that built its doctrine against the pull of things toward their center. The   \
  false GRIMOIRE. The one that floated.\
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  The omen is not subtle. The Judge is rubbing the bump on his head and she suspects he has already read it the same way \'97 he is a Bayesian, he updates on evidence \'97 and the      \
  evidence is: what defies gravity does so against the law. Not against a preference. Against Newton. Against the apple. Against the python who is already three branches further\
  on and does not look back.                                                                                                                                                       \
                                                            \
  She retrieves the apple from the dust. Turns it in her hand.                                                                                                                     \
   \
  "The Antigravity Lich," she says, looking at the fruit, "defies the thing that just hit you on the head."                                                                        \
                                                            \
  She considers it a moment longer, then sets it at the base of the tree.                                                                                                          \
                                                            \
  An offering. Or a filing. She is not certain there is a difference.                                                                                                              \
                                                            \
  "We should keep moving."                                                                                                                                                         \
                                                            \

