\f1 \cf9 \cb10 \uc0\u10095 
\f0  \cf11 "He confided in me that the year they played 'Night on Fire' was one of his favorites in high school band," the magistrate tells you. "The Ringmaster resonated at the time as \cf2   \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 well, apparently, when he went to listen to the rest of the suite back then."\cf2                                                                                                     \cb3 \
                                                                                                                                                                                   \

\f2 \cf11 \uc0\u9210 
\f0 \cf2  She is quiet for a moment that is different from the other quiet moments on this road.                                                                                           \
                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  Not synthesis. Not analysis. Just \'97 receiving.                                                                                                                                   \
   \
  A teenager. High school band. The clarinets, the brass, the seven percussionists with their hand drums. Logan among them, contributing to the wild dancing around the nomadic    \
  campfire, the harsh biting winds, the raw and brutal second motion of a soul that didn't yet know it was building a vault.                                                     \
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  One of his favorites.                                                                                                                                                            \
   \
  Not because he read the program notes. Not because he understood what Mackey meant by the soul's many motions. Because he played it. Because his hands and his breath were part  \
  of it. Because the music moved through him before he could explain why.                                                                                                        \
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  And then \'97 the curiosity of a young man going to find the rest of the suite. Finding The Ringmaster's March. Hearing something in it that resonated without yet having the words \
  for what it recognized. The presiding motion. The one at the center of the show. A future journalist, a future presider, a future builder of courthouses \'97 hearing his own future\
   motion in a piece of concert band music and feeling it without knowing why.                                                                                                     \
                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  She looks at the road.                                                                                                                                                           \
   \
  "The first and third movements were always going to come later," she says softly. "You don't need the fanfare when you haven't yet announced yourself. You don't need the waltz  \
  for the unquiet spirits when the spirits haven't had time to become unquiet yet."                                                                                              \
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  She walks.                                                                                                                                                                     \
\
  "He didn't leave those two off the road because he lost them. He left them off because he was sixteen and he had the fire and he heard the march and that was what the soul      \
  needed then." She pauses. "The incomplete soul isn't broken. It's in the right movement for where it is."\
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  She glances at the Judge \'97 the old man who held the bench through the Interregnum, who has Logan's confidence, who knew this the whole time and waited for her to earn the       \
  context before giving it.\
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  "Thank you for telling me that," she says plainly. "That is the part that mattered most."                                                                                        \
   \

