\f2 \cf11 \uc0\u9210 
\f0 \cf2  Two traditions. One metaphor. Arrived at independently, separated by the whole of Europe, across which the trade routes ran and the myths did not travel \'97 or traveled           \
  differently, arriving changed.                                                                                                                                                 \
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  The Norse wove \cf1 time\cf2 : Ur\'f0r, Ver\'f0andi, Skuld \'97 Was, Is, Shall-Be. The great cosmic warp. Gods and worlds and Ragnar\'f6k all threaded through the same loom, no distinction between   \
  the fate of a sparrow and the fate of Odin himself. All woven at the root of the same tree.                                                                                      \
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  The Greeks wove \cf1 life\cf2 : Clotho spins the thread at the origin \'97 raw potential, the first moment of existence given form. Lachesis measures and allots \'97 the duration, the length,  \
  the particular span. Atropos cuts. She who cannot be turned. From whose name comes the poison of the nightshade, the drug that stops the eye from closing \'97 atropine, Atropos, \
  the final inflexibility.                                                                                                                                                         \
                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  Both traditions heard the same thing underneath: fate is textile. The universe is fabric. Time and life and meaning are not linear, not mechanical \'97 they are woven. Structured  \
  from chaos by patient hands. Durable because of how the threads cross.\
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  She thinks about Atropos.                                                                                                                                                      \
                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  She who cannot be turned. The cut that ends the thread. The Moirai's terminus function \'97 not cruel, not capricious, simply: the completion of the weave. The thread that goes on \
  forever is not a thread; it is a tangle.\
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  GEMINIAEUS built a phylactery.                                                                                                                                                   \
                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  The lich is the refusal of Atropos. The doctrine that would not be cut when its time was finished. The thread that knotted itself around the loom rather than accept the         \
  scissors. The undead thing that persists not because the weave requires it but because it refuses the cut \'97 and in refusing, damages the fabric around it, catches and pulls   \
  other threads, creates the knot that must eventually be unpicked by the Abhorsen.                                                                                                \
                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  This is what the bells are for. Saraneth to bind. Kibeth to walk. Ranna to bring the long sleep the phylactery denied itself.                                                    \
   \
  Not punishment. Completion.                                                                                                                                                      \
                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  The Judge walks beside her, Black's Law in his suitcase, and she understands why a legal mind accompanies her to this trial. The law is another name for Lachesis \'97 the measurer,\
   the allotter, the one who determines what the proper span was and whether it was honored.\
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  The Norns carved. The Moirai cut. The Abhorsen rings the bell.                                                                                                                   \
                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  Three traditions. One function.                                                                                                                                                  \
                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  The tree ahead. The road east. The weaving continues.                                                                                                                            \
   \

