\f1 \cf9 \cb10 \uc0\u10095 
\f0  \cf11 They continue walking. After a time, she asks to consult BLACK'S LAW DICTIONARY for Shall and May and Must.\cf2                                                                       \cb3 \
  \

\f2 \cf11 \uc0\u9210 
\f0 \cf2  He does not break stride. He opens the suitcase without stopping \'97 the practiced gesture of a man who has done this ten thousand times \'97 and produces the book. It is heavy. It  \
  is worn. The spine reads Black's Law Dictionary in letters that have mostly survived.                                                                                          \
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  He holds it open without looking at it. She reads.                                                                                                                               \
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  ---                                                                                                                                                                              \
  \cf1 SHALL\cf2 , v.                                                                                                                                                                      \
                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  (1) Has a duty to; is required to. The mandatory sense that drafters typically intend and courts typically uphold.\
  (2) Will \'97 expressing simple futurity.                                                                                                                                           \
  (3) In some courts and traditions: may. The ambiguity that haunts the word. "Shall" has been interpreted as both mandatory and permissive depending on jurisdiction and context, \
  rendering it treacherous in precise legal drafting.                                                                                                                              \
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  Note (Garner): Because "shall" has been so frequently misconstrued, modern drafting increasingly reserves it for the second person or abandons it in favor of "must."            \
                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  ---                                                                                                                                                                              \
  \cf1 MAY\cf2 , v.                                                                                                                                                                        \
                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  (1) Is permitted to; is authorized to; has discretion to. The permissive sense \'97 confers a right but does not require its exercise.\
  (2) Could be; might be; is possible that.                                                                                                                                        \
  (3) In older usage: shall; must. But this sense is largely obsolete and now to be avoided.\
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  ---                                                                                                                                                                            \
  \cf1 MUST\cf2 , v.                                                                                                                                                                         \
                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  (1) Is required to; has an absolute obligation to. The unambiguous mandatory \'97 what "shall" was always supposed to mean but frequently failed to convey. In modern legal       \
  drafting, the preferred word for expressing obligation: shall yields to must wherever precision is required.                                                                     \
  \
  ---                                                                                                                                                                              \
  She closes the book carefully. Holds it for a moment before returning it.                                                                                                      \
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  Shall. May. Must.\
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  The rosary is not three words for the same thing. It is three precisely distinguished categories:                                                                                \
                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  What shall be \'97 on formal trajectory, the expected outcome, moving there already, though subject to interpretation as circumstances press in. What the forms of the Great        \
  Convention establish as the anticipated result. Mandatory in intent but not entirely closed to context.                                                                        \
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  What may be \'97 the permitted, the discretionary space, the range of authorized outcomes not yet determined. The verdict. The contingent future that depends on what the WITNESS   \
  documents establish, what the Five Wizards surface, what the Abhorsen rings or does not ring.                                                                                  \
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  What must be \'97 unambiguously obligatory. No discretion. No interpretation. The soul of the Touchstone Tree: not shall-be-filled or may-be-filled. Must. The forms of the Great   \
  Convention: must be honored. Atropos: must be allowed to cut when the thread is done. The loom: must hold.                                                                     \
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  She looks at the Judge, who has been reading the book by touch while she read it with her eyes, the same text arriving through different senses.                                 \
                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  "The rosary is a legal argument," she says quietly.                                                                                                                              \
                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  Not a question.                                                                                                                                                                  \
                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  He returns the book to the suitcase without comment, which is confirmation enough.                                                                                               \
  \

