\f1 \cf9 \cb10 \uc0\u10095 
\f0  \cf11 https://snicket.fandom.com/wiki/Sebald_Code\cf2                                                                                                                                       \cb3 \
\cb10                                                                                                                                                                                    \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 The Sebald Code is a cipher created by Gustav Sebald and used by members of V.F.D. It is known to have been used in the inventor's movie script for Zombies in the Snow, but it \cf2  \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 is also widely used in letters between volunteers to prevent them from being intercepted.\cf2                                                                                         \cb3 \
\cb10                                                                                                                                                                                    \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 Instructions for Usage\cf2                                                                                                                                                            \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 The beginning of a coded passage is signaled by the ringing, or mention of the ringing, of a bell. The first word to come after this signal is the first word of the coded \cf2       \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 message. Every eleventh word after this first word is another part of the coded message, making it so that ten uncoded words fall between every coded word. This pattern \cf2         \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 continues until the first bell stops ringing, the message ends, a second bell rings, or a bell's ringing is again mentioned.\cf2                                                      \cb3 \
\cb10                                                                                                                                                                                    \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 Here is a simple example of the Sebald Code:\cf2                                                                                                                                      \cb3 \
\cb10                                                                                                                                                                                    \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 (A doorbell rings.)\cf2                                                                                                                                                               \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 Gertrude: This is a very pleasant surprise! Please come in, Bob!\cf2                                                                                                                  \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 Bob: How is Ebenezer?\cf2                                                                                                                                                             \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 Gertrude: He is very ill. I have to give him an injection every hour.\cf2                                                                                                             \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 Bob: That's a shame. He once was an example of good health.\cf2                                                                                                                       \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 Gertrude: For comfort he looks at old photos of when he was healthier. I took him to see\cf2                                                                                          \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 Dr. Sebald, but it seemed as if he was just talking in code.\cf2                                                                                                                      \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 (An alarm clock rings.)\cf2                                                                                                                                                           \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 Gertrude: He must need another injection. Coming, Ebenezer!\cf2                                                                                                                       \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 (All leave.)\cf2                                                                                                                                                                      \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 When decoded, the message in this passage is: "This is an example of Sebald Code".\cf2                                                                                                \cb3 \
\cb10                                                                                                                                                                                    \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 According to Lemony Snicket: The Unauthorized Autobiography, the Sebald code was used in the movie Zombies in the Snow in an attempt to warn Dr. Montgomery Montgomery that \cf2      \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 Stephano was really Count Olaf in disguise. It is believed, however, that Dr. Montgomery did not know of the Sebald Code, which explains his continued trust of the villain.\cf2      \cb3 \
\cb10                                                                                                                                                                                    \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 The Penultimate Peril\cf2                                                                                                                                                             \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 Another example of the Sebald Code can be found on page 67 of The Penultimate Peril. The passage is as follows, with coded words bolded.\cf2                                          \cb3 \
\cb10                                                                                                                                                                                    \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 "Ring!" cried either Ernest or Frank. "Ring! I shouldn't have to tell you the bell's your signal. We can't keep our guests waiting for even an instant. You can tell which guest\cf2  \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11  is ringing by the number on the bell. If the number written on the bell was 469, for example, then you would know that one of our Portuguese guests required assistance. Are \cf2    \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 you paying attention? The bell marked 674 indicates our associates in the lumber industry, as the number 674 means lumber processing or wood producers in the Dewey Decimal \cf2      \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 System. We can't make enemies out of important guests! The number 371 indicated educational guests. Please be nice to them, too, although they're much less important. Respond \cf2   \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 to all of our guests whenever you hear that ring!"\cf2                                                                                                                                \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 Note that numbers are counted as a single word even if they are multiple words when spoken. E.g. 469 is counted as one word, not as three words (four six nine) or four words \cf2    \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 (four hundred sixty nine).\cf2                                                                                                                                                        \cb3 \
\cb10                                                                                                                                                                                    \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 When decoded, the message in this passage is: "I can't tell if you are in or enemies. Please respond." There seems to be an error in the results, with fans proposing \cf2            \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 corrections:\cf2                                                                                                                                                                      \cb3 \
\cb10                                                                                                                                                                                    \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 "I can't tell if you are associates or enemies. Please respond." (Moving one word backward from "in" to "associates".)\cf2                                                            \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 "I can't tell if you are in or out. Please respond." (Moving one word forward from "enemies" to "out".)\cf2                                                                           \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 This might be an unintentional error in the text or it might have been intentionally put in to further confuse the identity of the speaker (while Frank might have meant \cf2         \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 "associates or enemies", Ernest may have meant "in or out").\cf2                                                                                                                      \cb3 \
\cb10                                                                                                                                                                                    \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 It is likely that the idea of using different forms of the verb "to ring" to signal the start of the code was taken from Edgar Allan Poe's poem "The Bells" from The Coded \cf2       \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 Poetry of Edgar Allan Poe (a fictional book that apparently references Edgar Allen Poe's experience as a cryptographer). Two excerpts from the book appear in Lemony Snicket: \cf2    \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 The Unauthorized Autobiography.\cf2                                                                                                                                                   \cb3 \
\cf5   
