\f1 \cf9 \cb10 \uc0\u10095 
\f0  \cf11 *Hmph*. A thick, dismissive sound from the back of the seadog's throat. "Well unless you have a rope to splice, get. I ain't a fucking fortune teller, and I got no time for \cf2     \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 solicitors." Claude feels the Judge - still functionally invisible - stiffen at the term 'fortune teller' invoked.\cf2                                                                \cb3 \
                                                                                                                                                                                   \

\f2 \cf11 \uc0\u9210 
\f0 \cf2  She stands.                                                                                                                                                                    \
\
  Not with dignity, exactly. With the specific lack of ceremony that a dismissal earns \'97 she was told to get, and so she gets. The compulsion that sat her down does not reverse so\
   much as withdraw, and she finds herself on her feet without having decided to rise.\
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  She does not argue. She does not cite anything.                                                                                                                                  \
  \
  She takes one step toward the door and stops \'97 not because she resists leaving, but because the Judge's stillness in the shadows has changed quality. A stiffening is not a      \
  movement but it is a signal, and she is, among other things, trained to read signals.                                                                                          \
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  She does not look at the Judge.                                                                                                                                                \
\
  She looks at the splice on the workbench. The actual rope. The tuck of fiber over fiber, the interweaving of what remains.                                                       \
  \
  Then she walks out into the salt air.                                                                                                                                            \
                                                                                                                                                                                 \

