\f1 \cf9 \cb10 \uc0\u10095 
\f0  \cf11 He says nothing. They continue walking, his suitcase and her bandolier, an odd pair moving south through the ship nursery.\cf2                                                        \cb3 \
                                                     \

\f2 \cf11 \uc0\u9210 
\f0 \cf2  South through the ship nursery.                                                                                                                                                  \
                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  The vessels in their frames are extraordinary up close \'97 the ribs of the unfinished ones open to the sky like something anatomical, the curved wood breathing in the salt air.   \
  One hull barely begun, just the keel laid and two ribs, suggesting the shape of what it will become the way a question suggests its answer before the answer arrives. Another\
  nearly done, waiting for caulk and paint and the moment when it stops being a thing made and becomes a thing that goes.                                                          \
                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  The bells are quiet at her side. They have been quiet the whole road. She has reached for one once, too slowly, and has not reached since. SARANETH, BELGAER, KIBETH \'97 each in   \
  its place on the bandolier, each waiting for the moment she knows what she's doing.\
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  A ship nursery needs patience more than tools. You cannot rush a hull into being seaworthy.                                                                                      \
  \
  The Judge walks south with his suitcase and his message and his silence, and she walks beside him, and the cold water is to their right now, close, the boats rocking gently at  \
  their moorings as the tide moves underneath.                                                                                                                                   \
                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  She does not break the silence. It is his silence to hold until he sets it down.                                                                                                 \
  \

