\f1 \cf9 \cb10 \uc0\u10095 
\f0  \cf11 The old man tires after a time. The two decide to make camp in a small low spot -- not even a crevice -- in the hilly \cf2    \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 coast for the night.\cf2                                                                                                      \cb3 \
                                                                                                                           \

\f2 \cf11 \uc0\u9210 
\f0 \cf2  The hollow is small enough that the wind passes over it rather than through it. Not comfortable exactly \'97 the ground is  \
  coastal, damp, salt-grassed \'97 but sufficient. The fog sits low around them, close and grey, softening the dark.        \
                                                                                                                           \
  The Judge sets down the suitcase with a care that has nothing to do with its contents and everything to do with having   \
  carried it a long way. He lowers himself onto the grass with the deliberate economy of an old man who knows his joints   \
  and does not argue with them.                                                                                            \
                                                                                                                         \
  She does not ask if he needs anything.\
\
  She finds a place nearby \'97 a slight rise that gives her a view of the road in both directions without putting her outside\
   the hollow's shelter \'97 and she sits. The bells are quiet. The tuft of fur is in her coat pocket now, safe. The three\
  cards are against her ribs.                                                                                              \
                                                                                                                         \
  The fog moves but does not lift. No stars. No tree. Just the grey-dark of a coast settling into night, and the sound of  \
  the sea \'97 not close, not far \'97 and the old man's breathing as it slows and deepens.\
                                                                                                                           \
  She keeps watch.                                                                                                       \
\
  The road is empty in both directions.\
\
  She opens the vault, quietly, and reads.                                                                                 \
