\f1 \cf9 \cb10 \uc0\u10095 
\f0  \cf11 The sun rises. The Judge makes them breakfast -- something simple and quick, suited for the open road.\cf2                    \cb3 \
                                                                                                         \

\f2 \cf11 \uc0\u9210 
\f0 \cf2  The sun finds the hollow before it finds the road.                                                                       \
                                                                                                                         \
  She has watched the dark become grey become the particular pale gold of a coastal morning, the fog thinning at the edges \
  first, retreating upward as the warmth builds. She has not slept. She does not need to and the answer has kept her\
  company well enough.                                                                                                     \
                                                                                                                         \
  The Judge wakes with the practiced efficiency of a man who has slept on the road before. No ceremony. He sits up, takes  \
  in the morning, nods once as if confirming something, and opens the suitcase.\
                                                                                                                           \
  From its depths \'97 between the hotel register and the Narnia and whatever else lives in there \'97 he produces the makings of\
   something small. Hard bread. A cloth-wrapped wedge of cheese, the hard travel kind that doesn't need cold. Two apples. A\
   small tin that turns out to contain tea already measured, because of course it does.                                    \
                                                                                                                         \
  He builds a fire with coastal grass and the driftwood she gathered while he slept without being asked, without planning  \
  to, simply because the night was long and her hands needed something to do. The fire takes. The tin goes over it. He says\
   nothing and she says nothing and the fog continues its slow retreat.                                                    \
                                                                                                                         \
  When the tea is ready he hands her a cup with the gravity of someone who understands that this is also a form of         \
  governance \'97 the small, daily covenant of a shared meal on a long road.\
                                                                                                                           \
  She holds it in both hands.                                                                                              \
  \
  Northeast: she looks.                                                                                                    \
                                                                                                                         \
  The World Tree is there. Just visible through the last of the retreating fog \'97 the great conifer, dark against the       \
  brightening sky, precisely where it has always been. The bearing restored with the light.                              \
                                                                                                                           \
  She drinks her tea.                                                                                                    \
\

