\f1 \cf9 \cb10 \uc0\u10095 
\f0  \cf11 He hands her an additional book from Logan's Library -- "Castaways of the Flying Dutchman" by Brian Jacques\cf2               \cb3 \
                                                                                                               \

\f2 \cf11 \uc0\u9210 
\f0 \cf2  She takes it with both hands \'97 the same hands that held the tea, the same hands that would have hands.                   \
                                                                                                                         \
  The cover: a boy and a great black dog, the open sea behind them, the ship enormous and dark in the distance. Already    \
  receding. Already gone.\
                                                                                                                           \
  She knows the Dutchman by legend \'97 the ghost ship that cannot make port, the captain who swore to round the Cape of Good \
  Hope even if it took until Judgment Day, and so it has. She does not know this book.\
                                                                                                                           \
  She opens it while the Judge stamps out the small fire and packs the tin back into the suitcase with efficient care.     \
  \
  Castaways. Cast off from the condemned ship before the curse took hold. Not drowned. Not condemned to sail forever.      \
  Simply \'97 cast off. Left on the water with nothing but each other, the boy and the dog, and whatever the Dutchman's     \
  passing had changed in them without their asking.                                                                        \
                                                                                                                         \
  They do not age. They cannot settle. They move from place to place doing what the road requires, and when the work is    \
  done they move again, and no one who knew them before recognizes them after, because the time has passed differently.\
                                                                                                                           \
  She pauses on a page.                                                                                                  \
\
  The Judge is ready to walk. He has not asked her to hurry. He stands with the suitcase, looking northeast toward the     \
  World Tree, and he waits.\
                                                                                                                           \
  The boy and the dog, she thinks. The castaway and his companion. The telepathy between them \'97 not mystical, just the     \
  particular attunement of two beings who have no one else. The shared knowing that doesn't require words.\
                                                                                                                           \
  She closes the book and puts it inside her coat, against the three cards, against the bells.                             \
  \
  She stands.                                                                                                              \
                                                                                                                         \
  Northeast: the bearing clear now, the great conifer precise against the morning sky, the fog entirely gone.              \
  \
  "Ready," she says.                                                                                                       \
