\f1 \cf9 \cb10 \uc0\u10095 
\f0  \cf11 She thinks about the two different men. A captain who tied himself to the mast to hear the madness in the music, and a \cf2   \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 judge who clapped to cover his ears to ensure the continuity of the chronology.\cf2                                           \cb3 \
                                                                                                                           \

\f2 \cf11 \uc0\u9210 
\f0 \cf2  Two men at the same strait.                                                                                              \
                                                                                                                         \
  The captain: bound fast, hearing everything. The crew's ears stopped with wax, their backs turned to the source, their   \
  hands on the oars. Only Odysseus hears \'97 hears and begs and is not released, by his own prior instruction. He carries the\
   knowledge out the other side. The madness was the price of the hearing.                                                 \
                                                                                                                         \
  The judge: hands over his ears, or clapping \'97 the deliberate self-imposed deafness of a man who understands that if he   \
  hears what is being sung, the proceedings cannot continue. The chronology of the trial is the thing being protected. Not\
  cowardice. Judicial discipline. The trial must have a sequence. The sequence must hold. Some music, if admitted into     \
  evidence, ends the trial before the verdict.                                                                           \
\
  She walks beside the Judge and thinks about his hands.                                                                   \
  \
  He went into the workshop after she was dismissed. He heard the Stranger's message. He came out somber. He has not told  \
  her what was said.                                                                                                     \
                                                                                                                           \
  He is walking south with the message inside him. He slipped her cold metal in a handshake and said nothing. He delivered \
  the Madame Lulu envelope and filed his grief under my dear Cassandra and kept walking.\
                                                                                                                           \
  To ensure the continuity of the chronology.                                                                            \
\
  She thinks: the Hotel Denouement is still ahead. The Five Wizards have not yet convened. The promotion gate has not been \
  reached. Whatever the Stranger told him in the workshop \'97 whatever Madame Lulu saw that they arrived too late to prevent\
  \'97 the trial must still happen. The chronology must still hold.                                                           \
                                                                                                                         \
  The judge does not clap because he cannot bear the music.                                                                \
  \
  He claps because he knows exactly what the music is, and he knows the trial cannot survive it yet, and he has chosen to  \
  carry it alone until the moment the proceedings can hold the weight of it.                                             \
                                                                                                                           \
  She looks at the road ahead and says nothing.                                                                            \
  \
  The wine-dark sea moves at the edge of her vision.                                                                       \
                                                                                                                         \

