\f0  \cf11 Obsidian PKM Guide: How I Use AI to Build a LYT Note-Taking System\cf2                                                              \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 Obsidian PKM Guide: How I Use AI to Build a LYT Note-Taking System\cf2                                                              \cb3   \
  \cb10   \cf11 How many notes do you have?\cf2                                                                                                     \cb3 \
  \cb10                                                                                                                                  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 500? 1,000? More?\cf2                                                                                                               \cb3 \
  \cb10                                                                                                                                  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 Now ask yourself: when was the last time you actually found a note you wrote three months ago?\cf2                                  \cb3 \
  \cb10                                                                                                                                  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 If you\'92re anything like me, the answer is silence. It\'92s not that you don\'92t take enough notes \'97 it\'92s that you take so many they\cf2  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11  disappear. You spend time writing things down, and they vanish into a black hole.\cf2                                              \cb3 \
  \cb10                                                                                                                                  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 The problem isn\'92t your memory. It\'92s your system.\cf2                                                                                \cb3 \
  \cb10                                                                                                                                  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 Most people organize notes by classification \'97 folders, tags, trying to slot each note into the \'93correct\'94 location. But \cf2        \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 knowledge isn\'92t a library book. It doesn\'92t belong in just one category. A note about \'93remote work\'94 connects to \'93team \cf2           \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 management,\'94 \'93productivity,\'94 and \'93work-life balance\'94 all at once. Which folder does it go in?\cf2                                   \cb3 \
  \cb10                                                                                                                                  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 I\'92ve been through this myself.\cf2                                                                                                  \cb3 \
  \cb10                                                                                                                                  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 When I was using Notion, I tried the PARA method \'97 Tiago Forte\'92s famous \'93Building a Second Brain\'94 framework that organizes \cf2     \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 notes by project. Simple to start with. But a few months later, when the project ended, those notes got buried in the Archive \cf2  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 folder and I never looked at them again.\cf2                                                                                        \cb3 \
  \cb10                                                                                                                                  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 That problem kept nagging at me, which is why I eventually picked Roam Research \'97 its bidirectional links don\'92t rely on \cf2        \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 folders, so notes don\'92t disappear when a project ends. I bought a five-year membership for $500 and even invested in the \cf2       \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 company.\cf2                                                                                                                        \cb3 \
  \cb10                                                                                                                                  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 On Roam, I started using the Zettelkasten method. The idea is solid, but building cards is exhausting \'97 every note has to be \cf2   \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 atomic, rewritten in your own words, manually linked to related cards. Twenty minutes per card was normal. When life got busy,\cf2  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11  I\'92d procrastinate until I was barely finishing one card a week. Even more notes never made it past a rough draft \'97 a few \cf2      \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 lines jotted down, then abandoned, never filed, scattered across the system as if they\'92d never been written.\cf2                    \cb3 \
  \cb10                                                                                                                                  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 Last year my Roam membership expired. I also wanted to bring AI (Claude Code) into my personal knowledge management and goal \cf2   \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 system, and AI needs to read local Markdown files \'97 Roam can\'92t do that. So I switched to Obsidian. That decision completely \cf2    \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 changed how I think about knowledge management \'97 when AI can directly read and write your notes, a lot of the manual work that\cf2  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11  used to be a bottleneck just isn\'92t anymore. But that\'92s a story for later.\cf2                                                      \cb3 \
  \cb10                                                                                                                                  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 The tool problem was solved, but a new one came up immediately: I opened Obsidian to an empty vault. How should I organize\cf2      \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf9 \uc0\u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472  \cf26 (153 lines hidden)\cf9  \uc0\u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \cf2  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11  as I go. To put it in perspective: during my Roam/Zettelkasten days, I finished about one knowledge card per week. With AI + \cf2  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 LYT, I produce five or more cards plus MOCs per day. Every article and every research session feeds back into the knowledge \cf2    \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 base \'97 the compounding effect kicks in.\cf2                                                                                         \cb3 \
  \cb10                                                                                                                                  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 Remember the question from the beginning \'97 when was the last time you found a note from three months ago? My answer isn\'92t \cf2      \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 silence anymore. Every note from the past three months to today, I know exactly when I wrote it, why I wrote it, what other \cf2    \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 notes it connects to, and how I can use it. Notes don\'92t vanish into a black hole because LYT gives them an address, and AI \cf2     \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 delivers new material to the right place every day. When I need something, AI follows the MOC and pulls out everything \cf2         \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 related.\cf2                                                                                                                        \cb3 \
  \cb10                                                                                                                                  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 When I sit down to write, Claude Code first checks the relevant MOCs for material, fills gaps with web research, and adds \cf2      \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 newly found knowledge back into the system as cards. Most of the PARA and Zettelkasten comparisons in this article were pulled\cf2  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11  by Claude Code from my Obsidian knowledge cards \'97 no re-research needed.\cf2                                                       \cb3 \
  \cb10                                                                                                                                  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 But after about a month, I noticed a gap: LYT manages \'93knowing,\'94 not \'93doing.\'94\cf2                                                   \cb3 \
  \cb10                                                                                                                                  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 My Atlas looked great. MOCs were growing nicely. But every morning when I opened Obsidian, I still didn\'92t know what to work on\cf2  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11  that day. Knowledge management and execution are two different things \'97 LYT\'92s ACE framework has Efforts (projects and areas),\cf2  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11  but it only provides a place to categorize them. It doesn\'92t tell you how to break down goals into daily actions.\cf2               \cb3 \
  \cb10                                                                                                                                  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 A lot of people use bullet journaling or GTD (Getting Things Done) to track goals and tasks, but I needed a longer-cycle \cf2       \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 execution framework. I ended up layering 12 Week Year on top of LYT \'97 compressing annual goals into 12-week cycles, with clear\cf2  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11  weekly deliverables and daily executable tasks. Now every morning, AI and I do a 1-on-1 standup \'97 it reads my 12 Week Year \cf2    \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 progress, scans the week\'92s tasks, and we discuss priorities for the day. LYT manages knowledge. 12 Week Year manages action. \cf2   \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 AI ties them together. I\'92ll write a full post about that system separately.\cf2                                                     \cb3 \
  \cb10                                                                                                                                  \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 But back to this article\'92s point: LYT is a great knowledge foundation. It won\'92t help you hit your goals on its own, but it \cf2     \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 will make sure none of your ideas ever disappear. Over time, the connections between them grow denser and more valuable. \cf2       \cb3 \
  \cb10   \cf11 Whatever you want to build on top \'97 a goal system, a writing workflow, project management \'97 you can. The foundation is solid.\cf2   \cb3 \
  
