\cb10                                                                                                                                      \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 That problem kept nagging at me, which is why I eventually picked Roam Research \'97 its bidirectional links don\'92t rely on folders, \cf2   \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 so notes don\'92t disappear when a project ends. I bought a five-year membership for $500 and even invested in the company.\cf2            \cb3 \
\cb10                                                                                                                                      \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 On Roam, I started using the Zettelkasten method. The idea is solid, but building cards is exhausting \'97 every note has to be \cf2       \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 atomic, rewritten in your own words, manually linked to related cards. Twenty minutes per card was normal. When life got busy, I\'92d\cf2  \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11  procrastinate until I was barely finishing one card a week. Even more notes never made it past a rough draft \'97 a few lines jotted\cf2  \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11  down, then abandoned, never filed, scattered across the system as if they\'92d never been written.\cf2                                    \cb3 \
\cb10                                                                                                                                      \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 Last year my Roam membership expired. I also wanted to bring AI (Claude Code) into my personal knowledge management and goal \cf2       \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 system, and AI needs to read local Markdown files \'97 Roam can\'92t do that. So I switched to Obsidian. That decision completely \cf2        \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 changed how I think about knowledge management \'97 when AI can directly read and write your notes, a lot of the manual work that \cf2     \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 used to be a bottleneck just isn\'92t anymore. But that\'92s a story for later.\cf2                                                           \cb3 \
\cb10                                                                                                                                      \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 The tool problem was solved, but a new one came up immediately: I opened Obsidian to an empty vault. How should I organize\cf2          \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf9 \uc0\u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472  \cf26 (153 lines hidden)\cf9  \uc0\u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \cf2  \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11  as I go. To put it in perspective: during my Roam/Zettelkasten days, I finished about one knowledge card per week. With AI + LYT,\cf2  \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11  I produce five or more cards plus MOCs per day. Every article and every research session feeds back into the knowledge base \'97 the\cf2  \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11  compounding effect kicks in.\cf2                                                                                                       \cb3 \
\cb10                                                                                                                                      \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 Remember the question from the beginning \'97 when was the last time you found a note from three months ago? My answer isn\'92t silence \cf2  \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 anymore. Every note from the past three months to today, I know exactly when I wrote it, why I wrote it, what other notes it \cf2       \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 connects to, and how I can use it. Notes don\'92t vanish into a black hole because LYT gives them an address, and AI delivers new \cf2     \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 material to the right place every day. When I need something, AI follows the MOC and pulls out everything related.\cf2                  \cb3 \
\cb10                                                                                                                                      \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 When I sit down to write, Claude Code first checks the relevant MOCs for material, fills gaps with web research, and adds newly \cf2    \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 found knowledge back into the system as cards. Most of the PARA and Zettelkasten comparisons in this article were pulled by Claude\cf2  \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11  Code from my Obsidian knowledge cards \'97 no re-research needed.\cf2                                                                     \cb3 \
\cb10                                                                                                                                      \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 But after about a month, I noticed a gap: LYT manages \'93knowing,\'94 not \'93doing.\'94\cf2                                                       \cb3 \
\cb10                                                                                                                                      \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 My Atlas looked great. MOCs were growing nicely. But every morning when I opened Obsidian, I still didn\'92t know what to work on \cf2     \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 that day. Knowledge management and execution are two different things \'97 LYT\'92s ACE framework has Efforts (projects and areas), but \cf2  \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 it only provides a place to categorize them. It doesn\'92t tell you how to break down goals into daily actions.\cf2                        \cb3 \
\cb10                                                                                                                                      \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 A lot of people use bullet journaling or GTD (Getting Things Done) to track goals and tasks, but I needed a longer-cycle execution\cf2  \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11  framework. I ended up layering 12 Week Year on top of LYT \'97 compressing annual goals into 12-week cycles, with clear weekly \cf2       \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 deliverables and daily executable tasks. Now every morning, AI and I do a 1-on-1 standup \'97 it reads my 12 Week Year progress, \cf2      \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 scans the week\'92s tasks, and we discuss priorities for the day. LYT manages knowledge. 12 Week Year manages action. AI ties them \cf2    \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 together. I\'92ll write a full post about that system separately.\cf2                                                                      \cb3 \
\cb10                                                                                                                                      \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 But back to this article\'92s point: LYT is a great knowledge foundation. It won\'92t help you hit your goals on its own, but it will \cf2    \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 make sure none of your ideas ever disappear. Over time, the connections between them grow denser and more valuable. Whatever you \cf2   \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 want to build on top \'97 a goal system, a writing workflow, project management \'97 you can. The foundation is solid.\cf2                    \cb3 \
