\f1 \cf9 \cb10 \uc0\u10095 
\f0  \cf11 The girl looks up at the Judge. He gives her a reassuring nod, that one you know so well. She steps forward, and gives you an \cf2    \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 anxious curtsy. "Hello, miss," she intones. The formality addressed, she relaxes slightly. "I like your pretty bells."\cf2            \cb3 \
                                                                                                                                   \

\f2 \cf11 \uc0\u9210 
\f0 \cf2  Something loosens in the Abhorsen's chest.                                                                                     \
                                                                                                                                 \
  She crouches \'97 slowly, the way you move near something you don't want to startle \'97 until she is close to the child's height. She \
  receives the curtsy with a small, genuine inclination of her own head. The formality honored.\
                                                                                                                                   \
  Then she looks at the bells the way the child is looking at them. Through the child's eye, for a moment.                         \
                                                                                                                                 \
  They are pretty. The metal catches the hearth light. The leather is dark and worn smooth. The small perfect shapes of them, each \
  one slightly different, hanging in their careful order. She has been carrying them all road and thinking of them as instruments,\
  as weight, as the things she might need at the right threshold. She has not thought of them as pretty in some time.              \
                                                                                                                                 \
  "They are, aren't they," she says.                                                                                             \
\
  She unclips the smallest one \'97 Ranna, the Sleeper, the gentlest of the seven \'97 and holds it carefully by the clapper so it cannot\
   sound. She turns it in the firelight so the child can see it properly.\
                                                                                                                                   \
  She does not ring it.                                                                                                            \
                                                                                                                                 \
  She just lets it be looked at.                                                                                                   \
                                                                                                                                 \
  The way the child looked at her, from behind the Judge's crease. Now the bell receives the same eye.                             \
  \
  Fair exchange.                                                                                                                   \
                                                                                                                                 \

