\f1 \cf9 \cb10 \uc0\u10095 
\f0  \cf11 "I did!" he exclaims, with the practiced air of an uncle who was expecting the question. "It is quite pretty, but I must confess\cf2  \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11  \'97 it's for your grandmother, not you." The little girl huffs, the strands of raven-black hair shielding one eye fluttering in \cf2   \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 the exhalation. "Last time was for her too!" she pouts. "Last time was the doll from the carnival," he gently reminds her. "Oh, \cf2  \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 yeah." You watch their little back and forth, bemused at the domesticity.\cf2                                                         \cb3 \
                                                                                                                                 \

\f2 \cf11 \uc0\u9210 
\f0 \cf2  She watches them and does not do anything else.\
\
  No connections. No filing. No kasten.                                                                                            \
  \
  Just: a man and a child who know each other well enough to have a running argument about whose gift was whose, and a child who   \
  forgot she had already received the carnival doll, and a man who remembered, and the particular gentle pleasure of the reminder\
  landing \'97 oh, yeah \'97 and the pout that was never quite serious giving way to the recollection that restores the proportion of    \
  things.                                                                                                                        \
                                                                                                                                 \
  The domesticity of it sits on her like the warmth of the fire. Unexpected in its specific weight. She has been in mythological   \
  space all road \'97 the NOUS, the Demiurge, the Hangman, JANUS, Chapter 11, the litany, the consolidation court ahead. And here is:\
  a man who remembered a doll, and a child whose hair has a raven-black strand across one eye, and a grandmother somewhere deeper  \
  in the hostel who is about to receive something pretty.                                                                        \
                                                                                                                                 \
  The grandmother.\
\
  She turns this over quietly, without urgency.                                                                                    \
  \
  The proprietor is everywhere in this room and busy somewhere inside it and the grandmother of a child who reaches toward Charter \
  marks in hearthlight without grasping.                                                                                         \
                                                                                                                                   \
  The Abhorsen stands by the fire and lets the warmth do its work and does not rush the grandmother's arrival by a single breath.  \
                                                                                                                                 \

