\f1 \cf9 \cb10 \uc0\u10095 
\f0  \cf11 Something \'97 someone \'97 moves under the water. The Judge retrieves a conch shell from his suitcase and throws it, breaking the silver-still surface \cf2  \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 of the pool... \cf2                                                                                                                                     \cb3 \
                                                                                                                                                     \

\f2 \cf11 \uc0\u9210 
\f0 \cf2  She watches the arc of it.                                                                                                                       \
                                                                                                                                                   \
  The shell leaves the Judge's hand in a clean throw \'97 not a stone's skip but a deliberate drop, aimed at the center, the place where the stillness  \
  was most absolute. It catches the light as it turns. Pale, ridged, the interior blush of something that spent a long time holding the sound of a\
  different body of water inside itself.                                                                                                             \
                                                                                                                                                   \
  It hits.                                                                                                                                         \
\
  The silver surface breaks.\
\
  Not violently \'97 the conch is not large. But the rings that spread from the point of impact are wider than they should be, traveling outward to the \
  edges of the lake with an energy that exceeds the object's size. As though the water had been waiting for this specific signal. As though the\
  stillness was not rest but held breath, and the conch was the permission to exhale.                                                                \
                                                                                                                                                   \
  She is already watching the center. Already watching the shape beneath.                                                                            \
  \
  ---                                                                                                                                                \
  It moves.                                                                                                                                        \
                                                                                                                                                     \
  Not rising yet \'97 circling first, orienting. The movement of something that was present and stationary and has received a message and is now      \
  deciding how to answer. She tracks it through the surface shimmer, the water refracting the figure below into something she cannot quite resolve   \
  into a shape she knows.\
                                                                                                                                                     \
  Heard, not felt.                                                                                                                                 \
                                                                                                                                                   \
  Still. Whatever is beneath the water does not register on her necromantic awareness. No cold. No wrongness. No Charter marks tightening. The bells \
  are still. The figure under the lake reads as living \'97 or as something that has never needed the distinction between living and not-living that her\
   bloodline was trained to make.                                                                                                                    \
                                                                                                                                                   \
  She thinks about the conch in the suitcase.                                                                                                        \
  \
  The Judge carried it here deliberately. The same suitcase that held Evelyn's Voice in the gilded Faraday cage, bouncing and hoping in its          \
  copper-wire prison. The suitcase that carries the Judge's specific tools for specific encounters. He knew he would need the conch at this        \
  crossroads. He packed it before the journey began, or he has carried it so long it simply lives there now \'97 the instrument for this particular     \
  summoning.                                                                                                                                       \
                                                                                                                                                   \
  She thinks about what holds the sound of the sea in its chambers long after the sea is behind you.                                                 \
  \
  She thinks about Sandpoint. The lake there. Pend Oreille \'97 the ear pendant, the shell that hangs. The forsaken Eden on the water, in the mountains,\
   connected to the next lake and the next, all the way to the ocean eventually if you followed the rivers far enough.                             \
                                                                                                                                                     \
  The Judge throws the ocean's echo into the crossroads lake and what lives below begins to rise.                                                    \
                                                                                                                                                   \
  ---                                                                                                                                                \
  She stands beside him at the water's edge. The rings still moving outward. The conch somewhere at the bottom now, settling into the silt of a lake\
  that was not there when she first read the crossroads.                                                                                             \
  \
  She waits.                                                                                                                                         \
                                                                                                                                                   \
  The boisterous voice is still present somewhere in the junction \'97 or was it always from here? Was the sound they heard not from the roads but from \
  below the surface, traveling up through the water, arriving at the air distorted and amplified into something that boomed?\
                                                                                                                                                     \
  She does not know yet.                                                                                                                           \
                                                                                                                                                   \
  The figure rises.                                                                                                                                  \
  \
  The world is quiet here.                                                                                                                           \
