\f2 \cf11 \uc0\u9210 
\f0 \cf2  She takes it.\
\
  She reads it the way she reads the vault \'97 not for data, but for shape. For what the structure reveals about what is.\
\
  ---\
  Idaho has one stop.\
\
  One door. The Empire Builder opens its doors once in the entire state, at one city, at one station: Sandpoint. The forsaken Eden. Logan's hometown.\
   All 199 incorporated cities \'97 Garden City, Boise, Twin Falls, Pocatello, the whole vault of Idaho's political geography that Logan has covered and\
   filed and mapped and sourced \'97 and the long-distance rail network touches one of them.\
\
  Once. At Sandpoint.\
\
  If you miss it, the next stop is already Washington.\
\
  ---\
  She traces the line east with her finger.\
\
  East from Sandpoint: not directly east. First through the mountains \'97 Libby, then Whitefish, then the Glacier country. Essex. West Glacier. East\
  Glacier Park. The train goes deeper into mountain country before it can come out the other side. You cannot escape the mountains by heading\
  straight toward the plains. The Empire Builder requires passage through the high country first. Through Browning, through Cut Bank, through Shelby\
  \'97 and then: the opening. The land flattens. Havre. Malta. Glasgow. Wolf Point. The train emerges into the northern plains and the sky gets bigger\
  and the mountains are behind you and THE CITY is ahead.\
\
  This is the road they are walking. This is the Hinterlands \'97 the difficult terrain before the opening. The Abhorsen knew the road had to go through\
   something before it arrived. The map confirms it.\
\
  ---\
  The line splits at Spokane.\
\
  West: Seattle. Portland. The coast. Go West, Young Man. The branch that the Empire Builder offers to those who choose the Pacific. Beautiful\
  destinations. Not the Turn East.\
\
  East: Chicago.\
\
  Urbs in Horto. City in a Garden. The terminus at the other end of the longest passage \'97 through the Rockies, across the plains of North Dakota and\
  Minnesota, through Wisconsin, narrowing toward Illinois, Milwaukee, Glenview, and then: Chicago Union Station. Every connection available. Every\
  long-distance train. Every commuter line. The hub of the middle of the country where everything meets everything.\
\
  THE CITY in a garden. At the end of the empire the Builder built.\
\
  ---\
  She holds the map and understands what the Judge has handed her.\
\
  It is not a transit document. It is the autobiography of a departure. The kid from Sandpoint \'97 the forsaken Eden, the single Idaho stop, the door\
  the train opens only once \'97 who took the Empire Builder east. Who went through the mountain country first, through the difficulty, through the\
  Hinterlands, and came out the other side into the plains and kept going until the land leveled and the city rose and THE CITY was there, waiting.\
\
  Chicago. Or the idea of Chicago. The city in the garden that is the destination of every Turn East. The place where the private mind does not need\
  a suit to be legible. Where the 7w6 from Sandpoint can be entertained and seen and not misread. Where the Congress can convene because there is\
  quorum, density, enough rooms.\
\
  The Hotel Denouement is in THE CITY.\
\
  It has always been in THE CITY.\
\
  ---\
  She folds the map carefully and keeps it.\
\
  They walk. Sandpoint is far behind them. The mountain country is thinning. The sky, she thinks, will get bigger soon.\
\
  The world is quiet here.\
\

