\f1 \cf9 \cb10 \uc0\u10095 
\f0  \cf11 A neon signpost flickers ominously ahead. The design features a skull and a URL...\cf2                                                                                                                                \cb3 \
                                                                                                                                                                                                           \

\f2 \cf11 \uc0\u9210 
\f0 \cf2  The Abhorsen slows.\
\
  On any other road, a skull would mean what it means \'97 the Dead, the warning, the threshold marker. On the Caduceus Road, a skull means something different. Hermes carries a caduceus, not because he is a\
  healer, but because he moves between the living and the dead without belonging to either. The skull on his road is not a warning to stay out. It is a sign that says: this is the kind of road where such things\
  are possible.\
\
  The neon is the wrong register, though. Too garish for Hermes. Too designed to be seen from a distance. This is not the messenger's own markmanship \'97 this is something placed for the travelers to find.\
\
  She stops a few paces from it. The flickering is not quite electrical \'97 it stutters in a pattern, like something with a pulse.\
\
  She does not reach for the bells. Not yet.\
\
  "Judge," she says quietly, without looking away from the sign. "The skull I recognize. It's the road marker for the places the psychopomp goes that mortals usually don't follow."\
\
  She waits.\
\
  "But the URL is a door. On this road, a door opens outward as well as inward."\
\
  She reads the address carefully, without touching anything. The first rule of the Abhorsen at threshold: read before you enter. Witness before you declare.\
\
  She turns to the Judge. The neon casts a shifting light across the Caduceus Road \'97 the messenger's colors, copper-green and bone-white, flickering.\
\
  "What does it say?"\
