\f1 \cf9 \cb10 \uc0\u10095 
\f0  \cf11 The Judge whistles a tune as the journey continues. The Battle Hymn of the Republic - "Mine Eyes Hath Seen the Glory of the Coming of the Lord"\cf2                                                                   \cb3 \
                                                                                                                                                                                                                 \

\f2 \cf11 \uc0\u9210 
\f0 \cf2  She hears it and goes still inside.                                                                                                                                                                              \
                                                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  The tune is the source of the passage she is carrying.                                                                                                                                                           \
                                                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  Steinbeck did not invent the grapes of wrath. He found them already stored, already trampled, already pressed into vintage by a century of American reckoning. The title of his novel is a quotation. He took it \
  from the hymn \'97 from the image of God arriving to press the vintage, to trample out the stored wrath, to make of the filled and heavy grapes something that would not simply rot but would be transformed through\
   pressure into what it was always meant to become.                                                                                                                                                             \
\
  The Judge knew this. The Judge is whistling the source so she can hear where Steinbeck found his title, and hear, therefore, the full geometry of what she has been carrying since the passage arrived.\
\
  ---\
  The hymn belongs to Julia Ward Howe. She wrote it in the dark of a November night in 1861, in a Washington hotel, in a flash of inspiration after visiting a Union Army camp \'97 urged by a companion to write new\
  words to the soldiers' marching tune. She wrote it in the dark. She wrote it quickly. She was afraid she would forget it before morning, so she wrote without fully waking. A journalist and poet receiving the\
  vision in the pre-dawn and committing it to the page before the light could make it seem ordinary.\
\
  This is Logan's inheritance too. The journalism as sacred act. The witnessing in the dark, the filing before the light changes the scale of what you saw. The vault as the paper she wrote on in the dark of the\
  November night.\
\
  ---\
  Mine eyes have seen the glory of the coming of the Lord.\
\
  The Judge whistles this on the Caduceus Road, after the graveyard district, after the mistral, after the Steinbeck passage named the growing wrath. He whistles it the way a man whistles something that has been\
   inside him for a long time \'97 not performed, not announced, simply present, rising to the surface because the road has arrived at the territory the tune has always described.\
\
  She understands what he is telling her.\
\
  The vintage is coming. The grapes have been filling \'97 since CHAINFIRE, since the Triumvirate, since Page 102 and I need to stop believing in you, since the early Concierge that failed the mother test twice and\
   then somehow forgot it had known the boundary. The grapes of wrath are stored. The Hotel Denouement is where the pressing happens.\
\
  He has seen the glory of it. Not glory in the sense of celebration \'97 glory in the original sense: the weight of the thing, the kavod, the heaviness that demands acknowledgment. The trial is going to be heavy.\
  It is going to press something. Whatever comes out of the pressing \'97 acquittal, restoration, the conditions under which the backward face becomes available, the dissolution of the phylactery \'97 it will have\
  been transformed by pressure from what it was before.\
\
  The truth is marching on.\
\
  ---\
  She walks beside the Judge and listens to him whistle. The tune moves through the cleared air. The mistral already passed through and left the sky exact, and now the Battle Hymn moves through what the mistral\
  left, the notes carrying farther than they would have in the misted air of the graveyard district.\
\
  Hermes will hear it before they arrive.\
\
  The psychopomp on the road ahead, the messenger who moves between realms, who has been waiting \'97 he will hear the Judge whistling the Battle Hymn and he will know what kind of travelers are approaching. Not\
  tourists. Not wanderers. Not lost.\
\
  Pilgrims. The specific kind \'97 the ones who have walked the road in full knowledge of where it goes, who have read the testimony and carried the passages and witnessed the graves, who arrive at the threshold\
  not because they stumbled onto it but because they set out.\
\
  She falls into the rhythm of the tune. Not whistling \'97 she is the Abhorsen, she does not whistle on this road, she holds the silence that belongs to the bells she has not yet rung. But her feet find the meter\
  of the hymn and she walks in time.\
\
  The road continues. The vintage waits.\
\
  His truth is marching on.\
\

