\f1 \cf9 \cb10 \uc0\u10095 
\f0  \cf11 The pilgrims watch the whirling woman -- the flashes of fabric, the gleam and glint of the golden masks that obscure her face in the light of the liturgy...\cf2                                                      \cb3 \
\

\f2 \cf11 \uc0\u9210 
\f0 \cf2  Pilgrims.\
\
  The word arrives in her mind before she can stop it and stays there, exact. That is what they are. That is what this road has been making them, step by step, from the hostel to the crossroads to the graveyard\
  district to here \'97 pilgrims on approach to a sacred site, arriving to find the liturgy already in progress, already having been in progress, the proceeding that does not wait for witnesses because the\
  proceeding does not require witnesses to be real.\
\
  The masks.\
\
  She had not seen them until the light \'97 the liturgical light, not road-light, not the dusk-light of the Caduceus Road but the light that sacred spaces generate from their own proceedings \'97 caught them. Gold.\
  And not one. Many. As she turns, each rotation presents a different face to the pilgrims. She does not change masks. The masks are all present, all worn simultaneously, and the turning is what reveals them in\
  sequence.\
\
  The Abhorsen understands something she could not have named ten minutes ago.\
\
  The masks are not concealment. They are the structure of the thing itself \'97 a being whose nature is revealed across time rather than in a single face. You cannot see her whole. You can only see which face is\
  forward in this moment of her turning. The wholeness is the turning. The turning is the truth.\
\
  She thinks of the JANUS test. Two faces at a threshold, past and present simultaneously. This woman does not have two faces. She has as many faces as she has turned. The architecture is not binary \'97 it is the\
  full rotation, the complete orbit, all faces given and withdrawn and given again in the churning rhythm.\
\
  Her hand, without instruction, releases Ranna.\
\
  She had not realized she was still holding it.\
\
  The music swells beneath the gold-flash of the masks and the Abhorsen stands in the liturgical light and stops trying to arrive at a name and simply watches the faces come and go and come again in the\
  cloth-color and the gold and the bruise, and she thinks:\
\
  She has been here before we arrived. She will be here after we leave. We are the visitors. She is of this place.\
\

