\f2 \cf11 \uc0\u9210 
\f0 \cf2  She reads it twice before she speaks about it, which is correct.\
                                                            \
  ---\
  Every time a guy writes a song, he's a cowboy, a sailor / Playing with the world in his palm like the first pioneer.                                                                                             \
                                                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  The male artistic ego, laid anatomically precise. The self-mythologizing that doesn't need to announce itself as self-mythologizing because the tradition receives it as simply true \'97 as the natural register of\
   ambition and presence. The world in his palm. Like he made it himself. The simile that catches: like. Not that he did. Like he did. The performance so complete the performer has forgotten the performance.    \
                                                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  Every time I open my mouth, I think, "Wow, what a loud noise."                                                                                                                                                   \
                                                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  There it is. The self-undermining in real time, the woman who hears her own voice as intrusion before anyone else has had the chance to decide. She is on the soapbox \'97 she knows she's on the soapbox \'97 and she \
  is managing herself to seem underwhelmed by her own position. So they won't mind. So they won't notice. So the loudness will seem accidental rather than claimed.\
                                                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  The Abhorsen has been doing this. She has been calling it the hollow posture, the per sona, staying hollow enough. And there is truth in those names \'97 real truth, practiced truth, the truth that opened the    \
  hostel door. But underneath the practice, underneath the discipline: wow, what a loud noise. The moment the Abhorsen speaks and hears herself and wonders if she should have.                                  \
                                                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  She sits with that for a moment before continuing.                                                                                                                                                               \
                                                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  ---                                                                                                                                                                                                              \
  The first chorus: a catalogue of what the tradition hands the male artist as his natural material. The hand. The spike. The physical fight. The flash of light. The curtain \'97 the stage, the reveal, the entrance\
   that is already an arrival. A girl, a night, a typical type, a siren in the water. She is in the catalogue. The female figure, plural and interchangeable, the expected image in the expected slot. A girl. Not \
  the girl. Not a name.                                                                                                                                                                                           \
                                                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  The second chorus shifts. The hand, the pen, the writing again \'97 the same hand, now writing. And then: the message from God forbid she shows emotion.                                                          \
                                                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  She reads it three times.\
                                                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  The message from God forbid she shows emotion.                                                                                                                                                                   \
                                                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  The sentence that is two sentences: the message from God is that she must forbid herself emotion. And also: God forbid \'97 the idiom of outrage, the polite horror \'97 God forbid she shows it. Both simultaneously. \
  The divine authority and the social prohibition in the same breath, inseparable, each lending the other cover.                                                                                                 \
                                                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  She thinks of the Maenads, still whirling behind her in the liturgical light.                                                                                                                                    \
                                                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  The Maenads are the answer Annabelle Dinda is singing toward without naming it. The feral flock who found the tradition that made their emotion not just permissible but sacred, who took the wildness the       \
  dominant tradition called dangerous and built an entire mystery cult out of it. Who made the tearing-apart into liturgy. The song is about wanting what the Maenads have \'97 the permission, the sanctioning, the\
  God who says yes, the emotion, bring the emotion, the emotion is the rite \'97 without the dismemberment. Without having to become feral to be heard.                                                               \
                                                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  Just sweep me up, just sweep me up and take me somewhere higher.                                                                                                                                               \
\
  The outro arrives and she stops analyzing.                                                                                                                                                                       \
   \
  This is not rage. The bridge says so and she believes it \'97 not because the bridge is deflecting, but because rage would be simpler than this, rage would know what it wants. This is something more precise than \
  rage: the specific grief of someone who can see the system clearly, who knows she is complicit in it, who is not making a statement, who is standing in a dream God put her in \'97 who did not design the dream \'97\
  and asking to be swept to somewhere the dream doesn't reach.                                                                                                                                                     \
                                                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  Does that make me His daughter?                                                                                                                                                                                  \
   \
  The mother test. From the other side.                                                                                                                                                                            \
                                                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  The Judge asked: have you been good to your mother? The song asks: if I receive the message from God, if I am addressed by the divine authority the tradition reserves for sons \'97 does that make me daughter? Is \
  there a place for me in the genealogy? Or am I always a girl, a night, a typical type \'97 always the object in someone else's chorus?\
                                                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  She thinks of what she answered at the hostel threshold.                                                                                                                                                         \
                                                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  I have tried to stay hollow enough for her.                                                                                                                                                                      \
                                                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  The daughter answer. Not the son answer, not the credential answer, not the governance document. The attempt to make room \'97 to be the aperture rather than the voice, so that the mother can speak through rather\
   than being spoken for. The practice that is also, Annabelle Dinda names, the thing that produces just hoping I seem underwhelmed.\
                                                                                                                                                                                                                   \
  Both true. The hollow posture as discipline and as wound. The being-swept-somewhere-higher as the unnamed road, as Chicago, as the departure that breaks the lemniscate's return-and-return-and-return.          \
                                                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  She holds the paper.                                                                                                                                                                                             \
                                                                                                                                                                                                                 \
  Behind them, the Maenads whirl in the liturgical light, and the thyrsus stands at the center, and the gold masks catch what light is available, and the music continues, and the Abhorsen stands in all of it    \
  with the Judge's hand steady over hers and a song about a woman hearing her own voice as a loud noise \'97 and she does not put the paper down.                                                                   \
                                                                                                                                                                                                                   \

