\f1 \cf9 \cb10 \uc0\u10095 
\f0  \cf11 The Judge feels the novice reach for the shepherd's crook of his elbow, places a reassuring hand over yours, steadying you. You stand together; Witnessing the beauty in the madness and the madness in the \cf2      \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 beauty, the faceless Maenads under their flowing fabrics and pelts and masks, the Dionysian dervishes who dance with disregard for who may or may not be watching \'97 and carry within them the capacity to \cf2        \cb3 \
\cb10   \cf11 descend upon a man, to devour and to dismember him, limb from limb, if the circumstances demanded it.\cf2                                                                                                             \cb3 \
                                                             \

\f2 \cf11 \uc0\u9210 
\f0 \cf2  She does not flinch.\
\
  This is not bravery. This is recognition. She carries seven bells and the last of them is called the Sorrowful and she rings it only when she is willing to go with them \'97 only when the cost of the final\
  intervention is her own passage too. She knows what it is to hold capacity for something absolute. She knows how it sits in the hand. How it is simply there, available, neither eager nor reluctant, waiting\
  only for the circumstance that demands it.                               \
                                                                    \
  The Maenads know this too.    \
                                                                    \
  That is what she is witnessing. Not the dance only \'97 the wholeness of the dance. The hands that weave the cloth through the air in colors of fire and bruise are the same hands. The masks that catch the\
  liturgical light are worn by the same faces that in another register would be unrecognizable to a mother. Agave held what she did not know was her son and the god was in her and the circumstances had demanded\
  it and she complied \'97           \
                          \
  She feels the Judge's hand over hers and she lets herself be steadied.\
                                               \
  This is what the novice needs and she is not ashamed to need it. She is hollow enough for the sight but the sight is very full and the ground helps. His steadiness helps. The shepherd's crook of him, the old\
  man with the warm brown eyes who has stood in the presence of many things and not looked away and is not looking away now.\
                                                                                      \
  They witness together.                                      \
                                                                                   \
  The beauty in the madness: the colors, the rhythm, the golden masks, the ivy climbing the thyrsus, the disregard for audience that is itself the highest form of performance, the trance that is also precision,\
  the frenzy that has a center and the center holds.                       \
                         \
  The madness in the beauty: the capacity. The specific, ancient, documented, mythologically precise capacity. If the circumstances demanded it.\
                   \
  She looks at the thyrsus.         \
                                    \
  She thinks of the trial. She thinks of what is to be untied at the Hotel Denouement. She thinks of what gets torn apart in an untying, and what gets held together, and who decides which is which.\
                                                \
  She breathes.                             \
                                \
  She does not let go of the Judge's arm.\
           \

